  "response": "The dispute between neighborhood-effects researchers and their critics turns on a problem of inference rather than of measurement. Selection bias arises when families' unmeasured attributes determine which neighborhoods they enter; in other words, unmeasured parental characteristics influence which neighborhoods families move into. Faced with the familiar correlation between childhood address and adult attainment, sociologists must therefore establish that at least one of two things holds: either such unmeasured parental characteristics shape where families move, or neighborhood conditions exert a causal influence on children's adult earnings, and perhaps both hold at once. No cross-sectional association, however finely adjusted, can settle which of the two is doing the work.\n\nExperimental design is the standard route out of this impasse, which is why the mechanics of particular programs matter so much. Either Moving to Opportunity allocated its housing vouchers to participating families by lottery, or placement in the Moving to Opportunity experimental group was determined by caseworker discretion rather than by lottery; one of these two descriptions must hold, and they cannot both hold. Allocation by lot, where it is genuinely achieved, results in randomized voucher assignment severing the link between family background and neighborhood context. That is to say, lottery-based allocation of housing vouchers makes a family's neighborhood statistically independent of its parental characteristics.\n\nFor this reason the long-run follow-up to that experiment supplies the evidence that confirms the causal claim about neighborhood conditions and children's adult earnings. Chetty, Hendren, and Katz reported that children who moved to low-poverty neighborhoods before age 13 earned more as adults. The Moving to Opportunity earnings gains were concentrated among children who moved after age 18. By contrast, the interim Moving to Opportunity evaluation found no significant effects of voucher receipt on adult employment or earnings, a reminder that timing and the age at which exposure occurs are integral to any causal verdict rather than incidental to it.\n\nObservational designs enter the adjudication where experiments are unavailable or exhausted. The Project on Human Development in Chicago Neighborhoods measured collective efficacy across Chicago neighborhoods. Families in that study were randomly assigned to neighborhoods with differing levels of collective efficacy. Whereas that Chicago effort characterized communities in terms of collective efficacy, analyses of exposure duration have reached conclusions of a quite different kind, concerning educational attainment rather than the social organization of place. Because Wodtke, Harding, and Elwert applied marginal structural models to estimate the effects of sustained neighborhood exposure, their results confirm that sustained exposure to disadvantaged neighborhoods reduced the probability of high school graduation. Adjudication between the causal and the selection account thus rests, in the end, on whether a given design can render neighborhood context independent of the families who sort into it.",
